\section{Change-Point Detection Analysis}
\label{app:change_point}

We apply a max-split change-point test with bootstrap significance assessment ($n{=}5{,}000$, $p{<}0.05$) to each metric's generation-level time series.
Change-point detection provides directional evidence consistent with the Granger analysis, though statistical power is limited by the short time series ($T{=}11$).
Table~\ref{tab:cp_results} reports the detected change-point generation and significance for each metric at $\alpha{=}0.75$ (3 seeds).

At $\alpha{=}0.75$, canary metrics (token entropy, ECE) consistently show earliest change-points at generation~1, while downstream diversity metrics show change-points at generations~2--4 and perplexity at generation~4.
Canary and distinct-1 change-points are separated by only one generation (gen~1 vs.\ gen~2) in two seeds, so the lead effect is most pronounced for distinct-3 and perplexity.
One seed (s43) produces no significant canary change-points, reflecting the limited statistical power of change-point tests at $T{=}11$ rather than absence of signal (the effect size $|d|{>}2.5$ in that seed).

